  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Additionally, we investigate the asymptotic behavior of the solution and demonstrate how it connects to a constrained variational problem. Key aspects, including the compactness of the equation's field of definition and the degree of the map determining the point of intersection with a Hamma map, are established through a series of technical lemmas. Moreover, we establish a link between the given equation and a modified version of the biharmonic Choquard type equation by demonstrating the existence of a link between their respective global normalized minimum points. This link relies on an analysis of the equation's degree changes at points of symmetry and asymmetry, along with a modification of a previous theorem concerning a type of elliptic Hamiltonian system. This framework is applicable to other types and forms of the equation. We outline a method for broadly applying this approach to a vast category of equations possessing a symmetric/asymmetric property. This technique is also effective in addressing questions concerning the multiplicity of solutions, as it provides a means to trace the degree changes of the map defining the point of intersection with a Hamma map. Consequently, this approach is capable of addressing multiple solution problems involving a wide range of equations. Furthermore, we establish that the given equation possesses a degree of $8$ in the field of definition determined by the homotopy-based minimax principle, which guarantees the existence of two normalized ground state solutions. Additionally, we are able to demonstrate the existence of a link between the given equation and a modified version of the biharmonic Choquard type equation by showing that the

